{"version_id": 52, "article_id": 6, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Science Needs Fair Rules When Countries Fight", "title_zh": "国家之间闹矛盾时，科学合作需要更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 145, "reading_minutes": 0.7, "lead": "Science has often brought countries together. But now, political problems are making research harder. Two researchers say we need fairer rules.", "lead_zh": "科学常常把不同国家连在一起。但现在，政治问题让研究变得更难。两位研究者说，我们需要更公平的规则。", "preview": "Science has long been a bridge between countries. Many people believed research could go beyond politics. But today, global tensions are gro", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科学是国家之间的桥梁。很多人相信研究可以超越政治。但今天，全球紧张局势正在加剧。这正在改变科学家合作的方式。一个大问题是，科学家所在的大学现在表明了他们的政治立场。以前并不是这样。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 42, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/6/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 65.675, "size_bytes": 397872, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 165, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/52.json", "paragraphs": ["Science has long been a bridge between countries. Many people believed research could go beyond politics. But today, global tensions are growing. This is changing how scientists work together. One big problem is that a scientist's university now shows their political side. This was not true before.", "Two researchers wrote about this problem. Their names are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. Edeh works at Shanghai University in China. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They shared their ideas in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026.", "The researchers say we need fairer rules for global science. When countries fight, scientists should still work together. Their university should not be a political label. Science can help solve big problems like climate change and disease. But this only works if all countries can join. Fair rules can keep the bridge open."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科学是国家之间的桥梁。很多人相信研究可以超越政治。但今天，全球紧张局势正在加剧。这正在改变科学家合作的方式。一个大问题是，科学家所在的大学现在表明了他们的政治立场。以前并不是这样。", "两位研究者写了这个问题。他们的名字是 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。Edeh 在中国上海大学工作。Omoruyi 在中国金华的浙江师范大学工作。他们在 2026 年 10 月 6 日的《自然》杂志上分享了他们的想法。", "研究者说，我们需要更公平的全球科学规则。当国家之间发生冲突时，科学家仍然应该一起工作。他们的大学不应该成为政治标签。科学可以帮助解决气候变化和疾病等大问题。但只有所有国家都能参与，这才能实现。公平的规则可以让这座桥梁保持开放。"], "body": "Science has long been a bridge between countries. Many people believed research could go beyond politics. But today, global tensions are growing. This is changing how scientists work together. One big problem is that a scientist's university now shows their political side. This was not true before.\n\nTwo researchers wrote about this problem. Their names are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. Edeh works at Shanghai University in China. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They shared their ideas in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026.\n\nThe researchers say we need fairer rules for global science. When countries fight, scientists should still work together. Their university should not be a political label. Science can help solve big problems like climate change and disease. But this only works if all countries can join. Fair rules can keep the bridge open.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科学是国家之间的桥梁。很多人相信研究可以超越政治。但今天，全球紧张局势正在加剧。这正在改变科学家合作的方式。一个大问题是，科学家所在的大学现在表明了他们的政治立场。以前并不是这样。\n\n两位研究者写了这个问题。他们的名字是 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。Edeh 在中国上海大学工作。Omoruyi 在中国金华的浙江师范大学工作。他们在 2026 年 10 月 6 日的《自然》杂志上分享了他们的想法。\n\n研究者说，我们需要更公平的全球科学规则。当国家之间发生冲突时，科学家仍然应该一起工作。他们的大学不应该成为政治标签。科学可以帮助解决气候变化和疾病等大问题。但只有所有国家都能参与，这才能实现。公平的规则可以让这座桥梁保持开放。", "text": "Science has long been a bridge between countries. Many people believed research could go beyond politics. But today, global tensions are growing. This is changing how scientists work together. One big problem is that a scientist's university now shows their political side. This was not true before. Two researchers wrote about this problem. Their names are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. Edeh works at Shanghai University in China. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They shared their ideas in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026. The researchers say we need fairer rules for global science. When countries fight, scientists should still work together. Their university should not be a political label. Science can help solve big problems like climate change and disease. But this only works if all countries can join. Fair rules can keep the bridge open.", "vocab": [{"word": "collaboration", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作", "note": "常指一起工作，比如科学合作"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "国家之间的政治关系"}, {"word": "tension", "pos": "noun", "zh": "紧张局势", "note": "常指国家或人之间的紧张关系"}, {"word": "institutional affiliation", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "机构隶属关系", "note": "指你属于哪个大学或机构"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "代表，标志", "note": "这里指大学成了政治身份的标志"}, {"word": "fairer", "pos": "adj", "zh": "更公平的", "note": "fair 的比较级"}, {"word": "global", "pos": "adj", "zh": "全球的", "note": "指全世界"}, {"word": "journal", "pos": "noun", "zh": "期刊，杂志", "note": "这里指《自然》杂志"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any time\nLearn more\nSubscribe to this journal\nReceive 52 print issues and online access\n$199.00 per year\nonly $3.83 per issue\nLearn more\nRent or buy this article\nPrices vary by article type\nfrom\n$1.95\nto\n$39.95\nLearn more\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\nNature\n658\n, 572 (2026)\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nCompeting Interests\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\nRelated Articles\nAcademic boycotts: data hint at shifting research partnerships\nGeopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink research collaboration\nGlobal geopolitics should not stall science — 5 ways to push back\nSubjects\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nLatest on:\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nHow to mobilize the agile science needed in a crisis\nEditorial\n06 OCT 26\nRising numbers of Asian and African PhD students choose to study in China\nCareer Feature\n06 OCT 26\nPreprints must not leak sensitive research information\nCorrespondence\n06 OCT 26\nWhy smart glasses are about to create a major privacy crisis\nWorld View\n06 OCT 26\nAI biohazard hype must not divert attention from the real risks we face now\nComment\n05 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nRising numbers of Asian and African PhD students choose to study in China\nCareer Feature\n06 OCT 26\nCan we have class outside? Seven tips to breeze through outdoor lectures\nCareer Feature\n02 OCT 26\nI turn up late to meetings to dodge small talk. Am I wrong?\nCareer Feature\n30 SEP 26\nJobs\nProteomics Specialist\nBuild the science that shapes the future of human health. Application closing date: 08 November 2026 Join a place where ambitious science thrives...\nMilan (IT)\nHuman Technopole\nTenure-Track Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor\nThe University of Hong Kong We are seeking a motivated Tenure-Track Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor to join our Department of Pha...\nHong Kong (HK)\nThe University of Hong Kong\nGlobal Recruitment for Faculty and Researchers at Anhui Normal University\nAnhui Normal University sincerely invites outstanding researchers and academics worldwide to join its faculty.\nWuhu, Anhui (CN)\nAnhui Normal University\nScientific Project Manager\nHuman Technopole is hiring a Scientific Project Manager who will act as the operational lead of the European project PRE-CVD.\nMilan (IT)\nHuman Technopole\nMiguel Servet Researcher Position\nJoin IRBLleida as a prospective Miguel Servet candidate and develop an independent research line in biomedical research.\nLleida (Localidad), Cataluña (ES)\nIRBLleida", "fetched_at": "2026-10-06T16:56:16.643866", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Science", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.637, "sm": 100, "em": 637, "cs": 0, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "has", "s": 0.65, "e": 0.812, "sm": 650, "em": 812, "cs": 8, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "long", "s": 0.825, "e": 1.087, "sm": 825, "em": 1087, "cs": 12, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "been", "s": 1.1, "e": 